id: pm-web-25nv
title: "The per-IP rate limiter trusted a client-supplied forwarded address by default, so any caller could rotate a header to a fresh bucket"
description: "resolveTrustProxy returned one trusted hop when PM_WEB_TRUST_PROXY was unset. Every launch path this package documents exposes Express directly with no proxy: a docker run publishing the port, and npm start. Under that default Express takes the client address from the caller's own X-Forwarded-For header, so an attacker rotating the header draws a new bucket for every request and every per-route limit added by this change enforces nothing, while still reporting itself as enforcing them. The default is now to trust nothing, which fails in the visible direction instead: a genuinely proxied deployment sees all clients share the proxy address and an operator notices immediately. The hosted deployment behind Caddy declares its single hop explicitly in the compose file, and the variable is documented."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
tags[3]: "area:http","area:security","type:defect"
created_at: "2026-08-28T14:54:28.347Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-28T15:15:10.229Z"
author: claude
comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-28T14:55:13.653Z",claude,"Revert-checked: restoring the one-hop default drops the new test from passing to failing, so it measures the fix rather than describing it. The complementary test that opts into one hop and asserts the limiter follows the real client behind a proxy still passes, so the proxied behaviour is unchanged. Fifteen tests in the suite pass. Raised by Greptile as a P1 security finding on PR 110."
tests[1]:
  - command: "PM_PATH=/tmp/pmweb-25nv PM_GLOBAL_PATH=/tmp/pmweb-25nv-g node --test --test-name-pattern \"rotated X-Forwarded-For\" test/rate-limit.test.ts"
    scope: project
    assert_stdout_regex[1]: pass 1
    note: the default must resist header rotation
docs[2]{path,scope,note}:
  src/rate-limit.ts,project,resolveTrustProxy default
  test/rate-limit.test.ts,project,the header-rotation regression test
body: ""
